meta {
  name: rate-service
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

put {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/guest/v1/orders/{{order_uid}}/service-review
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-Guest-Token: {{guest_token}}
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "rating": 4,
    "tags": ["friendly_staff", "quick_service"]
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.status === 200 && res.body.data) {
    bru.setVar("service_review_uid", res.body.data.uid)
  }
}

tests {
  test("responds 200", function () {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(200)
  })

  test("echoes the rating back", function () {
    expect(res.body.data.rating).to.equal(4)
    expect(res.body.data.tags).to.deep.equal(["friendly_staff", "quick_service"])
  })
}

docs {
  # Rate the service

Sets `service_review_uid`.

**The order in the URL is the warrant, not the subject.** What gets written is keyed to the guest
SESSION, because service is experienced once per sitting rather than once per order -- a diner who
ordered twice has not been served by two different restaurants. The order is what proves this
session owns something here and that the rating window is open.

Run this against a *second* order from the same session and you get the same `uid` back: it is one
row per sitting. That is also why there is no "which order do we ask on" problem -- the question is
not about an order.

Worth provoking:
- `"tags": ["tasty"]` -> `TX_REV_009` (422). `tasty` is a DISH tag; the two vocabularies are
  separate, and the distinct code tells a client which one it got wrong.
- `"rating": 0` -> `TX_REV_002` (422)
- running this before `admin-orders / mark-served` -> `TX_REV_001` (409)
}
